    "mask_seams": "For fixed depth and tick the mask is constant on finite direction cells. Its seams are dyadic constant-u conical surfaces and longitude half-planes, with codimension-two intersections and the origin as exceptional representatives. On open cells the weak derivative of m*f_K is m*grad(f_K). Across an oriented smooth seam it has jump measure (m_plus-m_minus)*f_K*n_S*H^2|S; use the locally BV product rule for the Lipschitz f_K=D_K-epsilon (or its sampled-distance approximation). No Clarke gradient is claimed at a discontinuous jump. The origin/pole ownership values alone add no three-dimensional distributional atom. Import the final raw field region before canonical exact CSG; raw negation is not presumed to respect arbitrary-field Boolean laws.",
    "analog_domain": "0<rlo<r<rhi; rstar,r0,Rs,ell,p_shark,p_Len,b,alpha>0; rc>=ell; |eps|<1; |gamma|<1; |a_phase|<1; theta in R; k!=0; |zeta|<B<pi|k|. Triangular profile c_p(x)=abs((x mod p)-p/2)-p/4 is continuous, 1-Lipschitz, piecewise slopes +/-1.",
    "analog_stages": "x=ln(r/rstar); s0=x+eps*c_p(x); rS=rstar*exp(s0); g=rc+ell*tanh((rS-rc)/ell); rg=r0*exp(alpha*(g-rc)); s=(ln(rg)-ln(rg(rlo)))/(ln(rg(rhi))-ln(rg(rlo))); t=frac(theta/(2pi)); lambda=(1-s,s*(1-t),s*t); Len=s+gamma*c_pLen(s); Theta=theta+a_phase*sin(theta-beta)*sin(2pi*Len); R=Rs*exp(b*Len); F=(R*cos(Theta),R*sin(Theta),zeta+k*Theta).",
    "analog_monotonicity": "x+eps*c_p(x) is strictly increasing: differences >=(1-|eps|)*(x2-x1). Exponential, chamber, eruption and log normalization preserve strict monotonicity. Len differences >=(1-|gamma|)*(s2-s1). R is strictly increasing in r. Theta_theta>=1-|a_phase|>0 and Theta-theta is bounded, so Theta is an onto increasing map of R for each fixed r.",
    "analog_inverse": "Recover R=hypot(X,Y), Len=ln(R/Rs)/b, then invert the increasing Len map, undo log normalization, eruption, chamber by atanh, and increasing Shark map. From principal phi=atan2(Y,X), n=nearest_integer((Z-k*phi)/(2pi*k)) is unique because |zeta|/(2pi|k|)<1/2. Theta=phi+2pi*n; invert its increasing phase map and set zeta=Z-k*Theta. Barycentric inversion gives s=lambda2+lambda3 and t=lambda3/s; retain theta turn for a flat barycentric view alone.",
    "jacobian": "On every open differentiability cell of the Shark and Len profiles, det DF=R*R_r*Theta_theta>0. At triangular corners the ordinary classical Jacobian need not exist (zero gains can remove corners); the continuous map remains strictly monotone and is a piecewise C1 homeomorphism onto its image. The determinant is positive almost everywhere. This is a parameter-coordinate determinant. For the reference helical body chi0=(r*cos(theta),r*sin(theta),zeta+k*theta), det Dchi0=r and the physical deformation gradient is DF*(Dchi0)^(-1), giving physical volume ratio J=R*R_r*Theta_theta/r>0 a.e.",
    "injectivity": "Equal Cartesian outputs give equal R and hence r. Equal planar phases give Theta1-Theta2=2pi*n. Equal Z implies zeta1-zeta2=-2pi*k*n. Since |zeta1-zeta2|<2B<2pi|k|, n=0; strict phase monotonicity gives theta1=theta2 and then zeta1=zeta2. Without the thickness bound, distinct turns can overlap in 3D.",
